        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A 20 lb cylinder holds about 4.6 gallons and runs a mid-size portable for roughly five to eight hours at moderate load. A 22,000 watt standby unit at half load can burn 2 to 3 gallons an hour, which empties a 250 gallon tank in three to four days of continuous running. Consumption tracks load, so the figures move with what you are actually powering.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do the arithmetic before the storm rather than during it. Hours per day, times days you want covered, times your machine’s published consumption at your load — then add margin. Households planning with a single spare cylinder are usually a factor of five short of what a three-day outage actually needs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then stretch what you have. Cycling the generator rather than running it continuously roughly halves daily consumption and a full freezer coasts happily through the gaps. Shed the big resistive loads, keep the machine serviced, and if you are buying, a dual-fuel model means one fuel running out does not end your power — which, over a long outage, is worth more than the energy density argument that favours gasoline.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And keep the cylinders somewhere sensible while you are at it — outdoors or in a ventilated unheated space, upright and away from ignition sources, never in a basement. It is an easy rule to break when a storm is coming and you want the fuel close to hand.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check your own machine’s published figures at quarter and half load before finalising any of this — manufacturers publish them, and they beat every generic table including this one.
          </p>
        </div>


